1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,000
They're the ultimate comfort food

2
00:00:04,100 --> 00:00:06,240
and the world's most productive crop.

3
00:00:06,340 --> 00:00:09,740
Mash them, bake them, freeze them.

4
00:00:09,840 --> 00:00:12,940
Just make sure you duck when you fire them.

5
00:00:13,040 --> 00:00:19,360
From the Andes to Ireland, from Idaho to outer space,

6
00:00:19,460 --> 00:00:21,760
they're everyone's favorite tuber.

7
00:00:21,860 --> 00:00:26,660
Chips, fries, even spirits, the gang's all here.

8
00:00:26,661 --> 00:00:30,160
So get off that couch and keep your eyes peeled,

9
00:00:30,161 --> 00:00:32,660
because it's the potato on Modern Marbles.

10
00:00:47,220 --> 00:00:49,660
Your mother always said it's the most important meal

11
00:00:49,661 --> 00:00:50,660
of the day.

12
00:00:50,661 --> 00:00:54,660
And with any good breakfast, there has to be potatoes.

13
00:00:54,661 --> 00:00:57,500
Few know this better than the folks at the original Pantry

14
00:00:57,600 --> 00:01:01,460
Cafe in downtown Los Angeles.

15
00:01:01,560 --> 00:01:04,900
My dad used to cook bacon, ham, potatoes.

16
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:06,400
That was every morning.

17
00:01:06,500 --> 00:01:07,900
Reminds me of being at home.

18
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:11,420
You know, you always want potatoes with breakfast.

19
00:01:11,520 --> 00:01:16,420
Established in 1924, the pantry serves breakfast 24-7,

20
00:01:16,520 --> 00:01:19,220
365 days a year.

21
00:01:19,320 --> 00:01:21,820
In fact, because they never close,

22
00:01:21,821 --> 00:01:23,820
There's no lock in the front door.

23
00:01:26,260 --> 00:01:30,560
Their potatoes are fresh and the demand constant.

24
00:01:30,660 --> 00:01:35,360
Each spot is peeled and steamed, not boiled.

25
00:01:35,460 --> 00:01:39,140
That's their secret to both great breakfast potatoes

26
00:01:39,240 --> 00:01:42,780
and, if you're staying for lunch or dinner,

27
00:01:42,880 --> 00:01:44,940
the best mashed potatoes.

28
00:01:45,040 --> 00:01:47,780
We do country potatoes for breakfast with a grill,

29
00:01:47,880 --> 00:01:49,740
and then we do mashed potatoes.

30
00:01:49,741 --> 00:01:55,740
We do 20 cases a day, which is 1,000 pounds of potatoes.

31
00:01:55,741 --> 00:01:59,480
That's more than 180 tons a year.

32
00:01:59,481 --> 00:02:03,980
Not bad, given the entire joint only seats 60 people at a time.

33
00:02:03,981 --> 00:02:05,860
Everything comes with potatoes.

34
00:02:05,861 --> 00:02:06,860
It's comfort food.

35
00:02:06,861 --> 00:02:08,160
People really like comfort food.

36
00:02:08,161 --> 00:02:09,160
It's not fancy.

37
00:02:09,161 --> 00:02:10,160
It's not trendy.

38
00:02:10,161 --> 00:02:12,860
It's just people like it.

39
00:02:12,861 --> 00:02:14,860
Not only are these potatoes popular,

40
00:02:14,861 --> 00:02:17,860
they also pack a nutritional wallop.

41
00:02:17,861 --> 00:02:20,460
Every fresh potato contains much of your daily allotment

42
00:02:20,560 --> 00:02:25,560
of nutrients, especially vitamin C, iron, all of your vitamin

43
00:02:25,660 --> 00:02:28,600
Bs, and potassium to boot.

44
00:02:28,700 --> 00:02:30,200
Stuck on a desert island?

45
00:02:30,300 --> 00:02:32,500
You'd do OK with potatoes.

46
00:02:32,600 --> 00:02:36,920
But for all this good stuff, it's the taste that counts.

47
00:02:37,020 --> 00:02:40,220
Maybe that's why the average American consumes more than 140

48
00:02:40,320 --> 00:02:42,660
pounds of potatoes each year.

49
00:02:42,760 --> 00:02:44,660
And we're not alone.

50
00:02:44,661 --> 00:02:49,400
World potato consumption has doubled since 1993.

51
00:02:49,500 --> 00:02:52,000
Today, China leads the world's potato harvest,

52
00:02:52,100 --> 00:02:54,300
followed by India and Russia.

53
00:02:54,400 --> 00:02:57,300
America ranks only fifth in total output.

54
00:02:57,400 --> 00:03:02,020
Add it all up, and the simple spud is an international star.

55
00:03:02,120 --> 00:03:04,720
Potatoes are a very basic staple in everyone's diet

56
00:03:04,820 --> 00:03:07,520
because they can be produced in many areas.

57
00:03:07,620 --> 00:03:09,920
They can be stored for long periods of time

58
00:03:10,020 --> 00:03:13,620
and provide a very good base to any diet.

59
00:03:13,621 --> 00:03:16,620
and you can do many things with them.

60
00:03:16,621 --> 00:03:18,620
No doubt one of the most popular

61
00:03:18,621 --> 00:03:20,620
is the potato chip.

62
00:03:20,621 --> 00:03:25,620
Americans consume 1.5 billion pounds of chips every year.

63
00:03:25,621 --> 00:03:29,620
18 million pounds go during the Super Bowl alone,

64
00:03:29,621 --> 00:03:33,620
but not every chip is created equal.

65
00:03:33,621 --> 00:03:36,620
We're the first people to really think of potato chips

66
00:03:36,621 --> 00:03:39,620
as something other than really the cheapest possible ingredients

67
00:03:39,621 --> 00:03:42,620
you could get, and we think elevated the lowly potatoes

68
00:03:42,621 --> 00:03:47,920
potato to its rightful status as a gourmet potato chip.

69
00:03:48,020 --> 00:03:52,260
Since it was founded in 1978, Kettle Foods of Salem,

70
00:03:52,360 --> 00:03:57,260
Oregon has produced more than 30 different potato chip flavors,

71
00:03:57,360 --> 00:04:01,880
including spicy Thai, New York cheddar,

72
00:04:01,980 --> 00:04:04,480
and yogurt and green onion.

73
00:04:04,580 --> 00:04:06,780
But no matter the flavor, the process

74
00:04:06,880 --> 00:04:09,680
begins with local Oregon and Washington state potatoes.

75
00:04:12,620 --> 00:04:16,560
As you can see, we use many different sizes of potatoes.

76
00:04:16,660 --> 00:04:20,100
We're looking for interesting-looking potatoes,

77
00:04:20,200 --> 00:04:22,600
interesting-looking potatoes, different shapes

78
00:04:22,700 --> 00:04:25,840
to make great-looking chips.

79
00:04:25,940 --> 00:04:28,940
After an inspection and wash,

80
00:04:29,040 --> 00:04:32,640
the potatoes are weighed and separated into batches

81
00:04:32,740 --> 00:04:36,440
that fall into a high-speed slicer.

82
00:04:36,540 --> 00:04:38,280
This is a slicer.

83
00:04:38,380 --> 00:04:42,620
This is what slices the potatoes into potato chips.

84
00:04:42,621 --> 00:04:44,620
The potato comes down here by gravity.

85
00:04:44,621 --> 00:04:46,760
This is spinning around very fast.

86
00:04:46,761 --> 00:04:49,160
Centrifugal force forces the potato out,

87
00:04:49,161 --> 00:04:52,460
and it slices the potatoes at all different angles,

88
00:04:52,461 --> 00:04:55,260
all different sizes.

89
00:04:55,261 --> 00:04:57,160
Unlike most chip makers,

90
00:04:57,161 --> 00:05:00,280
kettle doesn't remove the peels before cooking.

91
00:05:00,281 --> 00:05:04,680
We think the peels add flavor and also some nutrients.

92
00:05:04,681 --> 00:05:06,380
Seconds after they're sliced,

93
00:05:06,381 --> 00:05:09,920
the chips enter a fryer filled with safflower oil.

94
00:05:09,921 --> 00:05:13,060
The exact temperature remains a trade secret.

95
00:05:13,160 --> 00:05:15,620
The steam you see coming out of the chips,

96
00:05:15,720 --> 00:05:19,520
potatoes are about 75% water, just like we are.

97
00:05:19,620 --> 00:05:23,960
And so when slices get fried, a lot of steam comes up at first.

98
00:05:24,060 --> 00:05:26,500
So that's one of the ways we know the chips are getting

99
00:05:26,600 --> 00:05:29,740
done is when there's not as much steam.

100
00:05:29,840 --> 00:05:31,880
At each station, workers move the conveyor

101
00:05:31,980 --> 00:05:34,980
from one fryer to the other, allowing

102
00:05:35,080 --> 00:05:38,780
them to load one batch of potatoes from the slicer

103
00:05:38,781 --> 00:05:41,620
while another cooks.

104
00:05:41,720 --> 00:05:43,820
Cooking by the batch really makes

105
00:05:43,920 --> 00:05:45,280
a different type of potato chip.

106
00:05:45,380 --> 00:05:46,220
It's a little thicker.

107
00:05:46,320 --> 00:05:51,420
It's a little crunchier, a little more homegrown taste.

108
00:05:51,520 --> 00:05:54,420
At this stage, all the chips taste the same,

109
00:05:54,520 --> 00:05:57,040
like potatoes straight up.

110
00:05:57,140 --> 00:05:59,240
The twist comes after they're cooled and inspected.

111
00:06:03,180 --> 00:06:05,680
So the chips are now going through the seasoning drum.

112
00:06:05,780 --> 00:06:07,740
That's what this spinning drum is.

113
00:06:07,741 --> 00:06:09,980
The chips fall through here.

114
00:06:09,981 --> 00:06:12,880
As they fall through, the seasoning

115
00:06:12,881 --> 00:06:17,580
comes out of a little tube and is applied at a specific ratio.

116
00:06:17,581 --> 00:06:20,680
So the chips go up here.

117
00:06:20,681 --> 00:06:23,920
Under the scales, these are 16 scales.

118
00:06:23,921 --> 00:06:26,420
The computer down below knows how many chips

119
00:06:26,421 --> 00:06:28,420
are in each of these 16 scales

120
00:06:28,421 --> 00:06:30,460
and releases just the right amount

121
00:06:30,461 --> 00:06:33,160
to fill, in this case, 9 ounces.

122
00:06:33,161 --> 00:06:35,560
This is our New York cheddar with herbs flavor.

123
00:06:37,740 --> 00:06:39,240
Perfect.

124
00:06:39,241 --> 00:06:46,120
Today, Kettle manufactures 22 different flavors.

125
00:06:46,121 --> 00:06:50,360
Yet in the more than 30 years since the company began,

126
00:06:50,361 --> 00:06:55,100
one thing has remained constant, the potato itself.

127
00:06:55,101 --> 00:06:57,700
We think potato is fantastic.

128
00:06:57,701 --> 00:07:00,500
Potatoes have served us very well,

129
00:07:00,501 --> 00:07:03,740
and we think we've helped potatoes, too.

130
00:07:03,741 --> 00:07:05,840
Potatoes have served us very well,

131
00:07:05,940 --> 00:07:07,680
and we think we've helped potatoes too.

132
00:07:07,780 --> 00:07:10,880
We think that all potatoes really secretly

133
00:07:10,980 --> 00:07:14,980
yearn to become Kettlebran potato chips.

134
00:07:15,080 --> 00:07:19,180
Whether for everyday snacking or a Thanksgiving feast,

135
00:07:19,280 --> 00:07:22,460
90% of all potatoes grown in the United States

136
00:07:22,560 --> 00:07:25,660
come in three basic varieties.

137
00:07:25,760 --> 00:07:29,500
The russet, often your standard baked potato,

138
00:07:29,501 --> 00:07:33,640
the round whites, and the round reds.

139
00:07:33,740 --> 00:07:37,140
We're so used to them, it's hard to imagine anything different.

140
00:07:37,240 --> 00:07:41,340
After all, potato is just a potato, right?

141
00:07:41,440 --> 00:07:44,140
Potatoes have changed over the years.

142
00:07:44,240 --> 00:07:46,080
Right now, this is the kind of potato

143
00:07:46,180 --> 00:07:47,720
you'll often see in the market.

144
00:07:47,820 --> 00:07:52,060
And it is well-suited to be eaten either as a baked potato

145
00:07:52,160 --> 00:07:55,660
or to make French fries by slicing it.

146
00:07:55,661 --> 00:07:59,200
But this is a relatively modern type of potato

147
00:07:59,300 --> 00:08:02,860
with a relatively modern use.

148
00:08:02,960 --> 00:08:05,400
Potatoes originated in the Andes Mountains,

149
00:08:05,500 --> 00:08:08,140
in what is now modern-day Peru and Bolivia,

150
00:08:08,240 --> 00:08:11,780
where people have eaten them for more than 8,000 years.

151
00:08:11,880 --> 00:08:14,380
Walk these fields even now, and farmers

152
00:08:14,480 --> 00:08:17,080
cultivate hundreds of varieties.

153
00:08:17,180 --> 00:08:19,660
Thousands more thrive in the wilderness,

154
00:08:19,760 --> 00:08:23,880
surrounded by legends and traditions.

155
00:08:23,881 --> 00:08:25,380
The ancient Incans, for example,

156
00:08:25,480 --> 00:08:30,380
measured time by the rate it took to cook a potato.

157
00:08:30,480 --> 00:08:33,520
The potato didn't reach Europe until the 16th century

158
00:08:33,620 --> 00:08:35,920
on the boats of returning conquistadors,

159
00:08:36,020 --> 00:08:39,320
fresh from South American conquest.

160
00:08:39,420 --> 00:08:43,140
At first, it was more curiosity than crop.

161
00:08:43,240 --> 00:08:46,100
I don't think that its food potential was realized

162
00:08:46,200 --> 00:08:47,880
by the people that brought it over.

163
00:08:47,980 --> 00:08:51,140
And it mostly resided in botanical gardens.

164
00:08:51,240 --> 00:08:53,180
When attention was paid, people

165
00:08:53,181 --> 00:08:56,880
found the potatoes malformed, even grotesque,

166
00:08:56,980 --> 00:08:59,680
fueling religious fears that the potato was more the devil's

167
00:08:59,780 --> 00:09:03,520
work than from the hand of God.

168
00:09:03,620 --> 00:09:06,100
Nearly two centuries passed before the potato became

169
00:09:06,200 --> 00:09:07,560
a staple of European diets.

170
00:09:10,660 --> 00:09:14,060
Today, of course, the potato has serious street cred.

171
00:09:14,160 --> 00:09:15,940
In New York, for example, local lore

172
00:09:16,040 --> 00:09:19,580
holds that no one gets elected mayor without a visit to Jona

173
00:09:19,680 --> 00:09:22,580
Schimel's potato knishery.

174
00:09:22,581 --> 00:09:25,580
If you could take a bite out of the Big Apple's history,

175
00:09:25,581 --> 00:09:28,080
it would taste like a knish.

176
00:09:28,081 --> 00:09:30,080
I don't think there's anything else like the knish.

177
00:09:30,081 --> 00:09:33,920
It's not like French fries or a baked potato.

178
00:09:33,921 --> 00:09:36,420
A knish is something that I think is just a knish.

179
00:09:36,421 --> 00:09:37,920
There's no other way to describe it.

180
00:09:37,921 --> 00:09:42,840
It's just a very delicious, flavorful kind of food.

181
00:09:42,841 --> 00:09:45,840
A schimmel knish begins with cooked potatoes,

182
00:09:45,841 --> 00:09:48,340
hand-formed and wrapped in dough,

183
00:09:48,341 --> 00:09:51,540
then baked in small batches.

184
00:09:51,541 --> 00:09:55,540
Along the way, spices, meats, and vegetables can be added,

185
00:09:55,640 --> 00:09:58,540
just as they've been for 100 years.

186
00:09:58,640 --> 00:10:00,540
From 1910, they opened this store.

187
00:10:00,640 --> 00:10:05,480
And all time, 100 years of making knishes, the same way.

188
00:10:05,580 --> 00:10:08,080
Nothing changed, nothing.

189
00:10:08,180 --> 00:10:11,200
It's traditional, protein-filled peasant food,

190
00:10:11,300 --> 00:10:15,200
with all the richness and flavor of America's immigrant past.

191
00:10:15,300 --> 00:10:18,000
You can make 100 times knishes.

192
00:10:18,100 --> 00:10:20,500
You put spinach, it's spinach knishes.

193
00:10:20,501 --> 00:10:23,000
You mix with the cabbage, it's cabbage greens.

194
00:10:23,001 --> 00:10:25,000
With vegetable, vegetable greens.

195
00:10:25,001 --> 00:10:27,500
You can mix potato with everything.

196
00:10:27,501 --> 00:10:29,500
Back in Los Angeles, where the power lunch

197
00:10:29,501 --> 00:10:33,000
is a daily ritual, leading chefs at acclaimed eateries

198
00:10:33,001 --> 00:10:37,000
like Mozza seek new ways to serve the simple spud.

199
00:10:37,001 --> 00:10:43,000
Potatoes find their way into our menu in a few categories.

200
00:10:43,001 --> 00:10:47,000
First of all, at the pizzeria, one of our very popular pizzas

201
00:10:47,001 --> 00:10:49,500
is a potato and gorgonzola pizza.

202
00:10:49,501 --> 00:10:51,500
And it's absolutely delicious.

203
00:10:51,501 --> 00:10:55,500
To gain a leg up in this highly competitive restaurant scene,

204
00:10:55,501 --> 00:10:58,500
Nancy Silverton and her head chef, Matt Molina,

205
00:10:58,501 --> 00:11:01,500
find a wide variety of potatoes at the Santa Monica Farmers Market,

206
00:11:01,501 --> 00:11:04,500
one of the oldest in the country.

207
00:11:04,501 --> 00:11:06,500
To be able to have that kind of conversation,

208
00:11:06,501 --> 00:11:08,500
for the farmer to be able to say to you,

209
00:11:08,501 --> 00:11:11,500
we're planting some more varieties, what do you want?

210
00:11:11,501 --> 00:11:12,500
What do you use them?

211
00:11:12,501 --> 00:11:14,500
You know, having that exchange is so important,

212
00:11:14,501 --> 00:11:18,500
obviously something that you would never get at a supermarket.

213
00:11:18,501 --> 00:11:20,900
Weiser Farms has been in the potato business

214
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:22,800
for nearly 30 years.

215
00:11:22,900 --> 00:11:25,300
What we do is we harvest them and then

216
00:11:25,400 --> 00:11:27,200
custom order size them.

217
00:11:27,300 --> 00:11:29,340
This is where we began talking to chefs,

218
00:11:29,440 --> 00:11:32,040
getting feedback from customers.

219
00:11:32,140 --> 00:11:34,240
And it's really defined who we are.

220
00:11:34,340 --> 00:11:36,960
And we love growing all these different varieties.

221
00:11:37,060 --> 00:11:40,160
As a farmer, you're always a small farmer trying to survive.

222
00:11:40,260 --> 00:11:43,360
You kind of have to do something different.

223
00:11:43,460 --> 00:11:46,700
Using a potato variety named Russian Fingerling,

224
00:11:46,701 --> 00:11:50,300
The chefs at Moza create one of their most popular appetizers.

225
00:11:50,400 --> 00:11:51,700
Rather than having French fries,

226
00:11:51,800 --> 00:11:53,200
because everybody loves French fries, right?

227
00:11:53,300 --> 00:11:54,600
You've got to give them their French fries.

228
00:11:54,700 --> 00:11:57,240
We do a dish where we cook the fingerlings

229
00:11:57,340 --> 00:12:00,940
in extra virgin olive oil and butter and rosemary, salt,

230
00:12:01,040 --> 00:12:02,140
of course.

231
00:12:02,240 --> 00:12:05,160
And when they're fully cooked, we smash them.

232
00:12:05,260 --> 00:12:07,660
We go ahead and just apply a little bit of pressure

233
00:12:07,760 --> 00:12:09,960
with at the palm of our hand.

234
00:12:10,060 --> 00:12:11,800
And we just want to compress it.

235
00:12:11,900 --> 00:12:13,260
But we don't want to break the potato.

236
00:12:13,360 --> 00:12:16,660
We just want to basically smash it so you can see the color

237
00:12:16,700 --> 00:12:20,700
of the flesh and the skin.

238
00:12:20,800 --> 00:12:23,300
And by smashing it, you get a really crunchy

239
00:12:23,400 --> 00:12:26,440
and then creamy inside potato.

240
00:12:26,540 --> 00:12:30,940
To the potatoes, the chef adds fried garbanzo beans,

241
00:12:31,040 --> 00:12:35,760
the herbs rosemary and sage, also fried,

242
00:12:35,860 --> 00:12:39,260
and a garlic mayonnaise.

243
00:12:39,360 --> 00:12:44,260
And there you have it, the fried potatoes

244
00:12:44,261 --> 00:12:50,700
potatoes with chetchi and herbs here at Moza.

245
00:12:50,800 --> 00:12:53,700
The full recipe is included in the US Potato Board's Year

246
00:12:53,800 --> 00:12:56,500
of the Potato Cookbook.

247
00:12:56,600 --> 00:12:59,000
But if that wasn't enough to whet your appetite,

248
00:12:59,100 --> 00:13:02,120
then here's a little something to raise your spirits.

249
00:13:02,220 --> 00:13:03,320
Potato vodka, anyone?

250
00:13:06,420 --> 00:13:08,660
World farmland devoted to the spud

251
00:13:08,760 --> 00:13:13,120
now measures an estimated 74,000 square miles.

252
00:13:13,121 --> 00:13:16,360
put all that land together, and it's bigger than all the states

253
00:13:16,361 --> 00:13:17,360
in New England.

254
00:13:25,660 --> 00:13:27,960
OK, let's harvest.

255
00:13:27,961 --> 00:13:31,340
Every fall, tens of thousands of acres of American potato fields

256
00:13:31,341 --> 00:13:35,680
are upended, and over 20 million tons of potatoes revealed.

257
00:13:35,681 --> 00:13:42,680
Idaho and Washington state dominate the market,

258
00:13:42,681 --> 00:13:45,680
but potatoes grow in every state in America.

259
00:13:45,681 --> 00:13:51,680
Here at Sturman Masser Farms outside of Harrisburg, Pennsylvania,

260
00:13:51,681 --> 00:13:55,680
they've been raising potatoes for eight generations.

261
00:13:55,681 --> 00:13:58,680
We're growing 600 acres of potatoes,

262
00:13:58,681 --> 00:14:04,680
and we're harvesting anywhere from 30,000 pounds to 40,000 pounds.

263
00:14:04,681 --> 00:14:07,420
per acre, depending on the variety.

264
00:14:07,520 --> 00:14:11,320
That's 24 million pounds for anyone who's counting.

265
00:14:11,420 --> 00:14:14,020
The variety here is Superior White,

266
00:14:14,120 --> 00:14:17,920
a round, all-purpose cooking potato.

267
00:14:18,020 --> 00:14:22,040
Every plant in the row will produce six to eight potatoes.

268
00:14:22,140 --> 00:14:25,940
We're looking at a strip of 108 rows behind us,

269
00:14:26,040 --> 00:14:30,040
and there's about 10,000 pounds in each row.

270
00:14:30,140 --> 00:14:32,340
Harvest is a combination of digging,

271
00:14:32,341 --> 00:14:36,380
of digging, then gathering the potatoes, which grow five to six

272
00:14:36,381 --> 00:14:41,120
inches below ground. Pulled by a tractor, a basic harvester

273
00:14:41,121 --> 00:14:45,820
uses rotating blades to separate any underground vines, while a

274
00:14:45,821 --> 00:14:49,020
steel shovel scoops the potatoes from the dirt.

275
00:14:49,021 --> 00:14:52,160
The harvester deposits them in a row for later pick-up.

276
00:14:52,161 --> 00:14:55,060
You'll see we're digging four rows of potatoes, and then we

277
00:14:55,061 --> 00:14:59,600
have a machine that will pick up those four rows, and it'll go

278
00:14:59,601 --> 00:15:04,800
separation system where it'll pick out the potatoes from the

279
00:15:04,900 --> 00:15:08,840
rock and clods and deposit them on the truck that's driving

280
00:15:08,940 --> 00:15:09,640
alongside.

281
00:15:13,240 --> 00:15:17,660
Once gathered, trucks that carry upwards of 45,000 pounds

282
00:15:17,760 --> 00:15:21,260
return to the main processing facility, where even unloading

283
00:15:21,360 --> 00:15:22,460
is highly efficient.

284
00:15:22,461 --> 00:15:27,460
Our hydraulic dumper can dump roughly 45,000 pounds

285
00:15:27,461 --> 00:15:29,460
in eight to 10 minutes.

286
00:15:29,461 --> 00:15:33,460
And we can do about 20 trailer loads per day,

287
00:15:33,461 --> 00:15:35,460
roughly 50,000 pounds per hour.

288
00:15:35,461 --> 00:15:39,460
Workers remove large debris by hand,

289
00:15:39,461 --> 00:15:42,460
then steer the load into a water flume

290
00:15:42,461 --> 00:15:45,460
to separate even the smallest rocks.

291
00:15:45,461 --> 00:15:47,460
The rocks are heavier.

292
00:15:47,461 --> 00:15:48,460
They sink to the bottom.

293
00:15:48,461 --> 00:15:49,460
They're transported out.

294
00:15:49,461 --> 00:15:51,460
Potatoes are full of water.

295
00:15:51,461 --> 00:15:52,960
potatoes are floating in the water.

296
00:15:53,060 --> 00:15:56,500
They travel around this rock flume, up to the conveyor

297
00:15:56,600 --> 00:15:59,700
here, and then back to the washer.

298
00:15:59,800 --> 00:16:01,600
A series of brushes gives the potatoes

299
00:16:01,700 --> 00:16:05,100
a good stiff cleaning, removing any residual dirt

300
00:16:05,200 --> 00:16:07,100
on the skins.

301
00:16:07,200 --> 00:16:09,540
Once this dance is complete, the potatoes

302
00:16:09,640 --> 00:16:12,480
are diverted either to long-term storage, fresh table

303
00:16:12,580 --> 00:16:17,460
stock, or commercial packaging.

304
00:16:17,560 --> 00:16:21,420
Solanum tuberosum, the potato, is an animal

305
00:16:21,460 --> 00:16:25,260
a botanical relative of the tomato, the eggplant, even

306
00:16:25,360 --> 00:16:28,360
tobacco, and the pepper, but technically not the sweet

307
00:16:28,460 --> 00:16:29,760
potato.

308
00:16:29,860 --> 00:16:31,700
Go figure.

309
00:16:31,800 --> 00:16:36,300
Up to 85% of the potato plant is edible, versus 50% of most

310
00:16:36,400 --> 00:16:39,020
cereal grains like wheat.

311
00:16:39,120 --> 00:16:41,620
But don't munch on the potato plant leaves.

312
00:16:41,720 --> 00:16:43,220
They can be poisonous.

313
00:16:43,320 --> 00:16:45,860
The potato is actually a stem.

314
00:16:45,960 --> 00:16:47,720
It's an underground stem.

315
00:16:47,820 --> 00:16:51,460
If you look at a potato, the eyes are growing points on

316
00:16:51,461 --> 00:16:55,860
Think of the potato as a large storage organ.

317
00:16:55,960 --> 00:16:59,400
From one of the potato eyes, the primary stem arises

318
00:16:59,500 --> 00:17:01,900
in search of sunlight.

319
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:05,000
Once it breaks the surface, the plant generates new shoots

320
00:17:05,100 --> 00:17:08,840
and stems to store its nutrients.

321
00:17:08,940 --> 00:17:12,740
After a dormancy period, the tips of the stem swell up

322
00:17:12,840 --> 00:17:14,780
to form new potatoes.

323
00:17:14,781 --> 00:17:16,780
potatoes.

324
00:17:20,780 --> 00:17:23,180
Cut a fresh potato into four pieces,

325
00:17:23,280 --> 00:17:25,320
and you either have the start of a meal

326
00:17:25,420 --> 00:17:29,320
or the seed necessary to begin four new potato plants.

327
00:17:29,420 --> 00:17:30,820
These are Norwich potatoes.

328
00:17:30,920 --> 00:17:32,540
They'll have to have a couple more weeks

329
00:17:32,640 --> 00:17:34,240
to be of adequate size.

330
00:17:34,340 --> 00:17:35,840
But this is the seed piece.

331
00:17:35,940 --> 00:17:39,980
We plant pieces of basically these potatoes chopped up.

332
00:17:40,080 --> 00:17:43,380
And from each of the eyes on the potato, this plant grows.

333
00:17:43,381 --> 00:17:46,020
is growing out of a single shoot from an eye,

334
00:17:46,120 --> 00:17:48,980
and this is the end result.

335
00:17:49,080 --> 00:17:51,520
In decades past, farmers planted, tended,

336
00:17:51,620 --> 00:17:55,320
and did much of their harvest by hand.

337
00:17:55,420 --> 00:17:58,020
And it was nearly always a family affair.

338
00:17:58,120 --> 00:18:00,500
In Maine, for example, school children were released

339
00:18:00,600 --> 00:18:04,540
from classes every fall to handpick the potatoes.

340
00:18:04,640 --> 00:18:07,440
I started picking potatoes at the age of five.

341
00:18:07,540 --> 00:18:10,280
I had two older sisters, and there were four of us

342
00:18:10,281 --> 00:18:13,980
of us in the families, 25 cents a barrel.

343
00:18:14,080 --> 00:18:17,480
And a barrel is four big baskets.

344
00:18:17,580 --> 00:18:21,720
In fact, this is an example right here of a barrel.

345
00:18:21,820 --> 00:18:26,620
So picking potatoes off the ground, putting it in a basket,

346
00:18:26,720 --> 00:18:28,840
and putting it in a barrel like this.

347
00:18:28,940 --> 00:18:32,140
And then you put your ticket on, and that would be worth 25

348
00:18:32,240 --> 00:18:34,440
cents worth of work.

349
00:18:34,540 --> 00:18:37,140
Today, not even driving the tractors or harvesters

350
00:18:37,240 --> 00:18:39,840
is hands on.

351
00:18:39,841 --> 00:18:42,540
Here at Green Thumb Farms in Freiburg, Maine,

352
00:18:42,541 --> 00:18:46,220
the machinery literally steers itself using the latest GPS

353
00:18:46,221 --> 00:18:47,520
technology.

354
00:18:47,521 --> 00:18:51,780
The tractor works just like an autopilot in an airplane,

355
00:18:51,781 --> 00:18:53,120
identical.

356
00:18:53,121 --> 00:18:55,660
Tiller-like, computer-controlled wheels

357
00:18:55,661 --> 00:18:59,160
attached to the tractor, guided across the terrain using data

358
00:18:59,161 --> 00:19:00,020
sent from satellites.

359
00:19:02,700 --> 00:19:06,540
The operator only takes control to bring the tractor

360
00:19:06,541 --> 00:19:11,640
around beyond the 180-degree mark on the compass on his turn.

361
00:19:11,740 --> 00:19:16,020
When he comes back beyond 180 degrees from where he was,

362
00:19:16,120 --> 00:19:18,960
he basically pushes a start or an enter mode,

363
00:19:19,060 --> 00:19:23,120
and the tractor knows where it wants to go on its next track,

364
00:19:23,220 --> 00:19:24,460
lines up, and goes.

365
00:19:24,560 --> 00:19:26,900
It's hands off.

366
00:19:27,000 --> 00:19:30,860
Prior to GPS, farmers cut their potato rows by eye,

367
00:19:30,960 --> 00:19:34,000
using a hedge or tree line as guide.

368
00:19:34,100 --> 00:19:36,140
Many were remarkably accurate.

369
00:19:36,141 --> 00:19:38,340
but not perfect.

370
00:19:38,341 --> 00:19:40,680
Even the slightest variation caused by terrain

371
00:19:40,681 --> 00:19:46,680
or simple human error was amplified as more rows were added.

372
00:19:46,681 --> 00:19:49,480
GPS changed all that.

373
00:19:49,481 --> 00:19:53,100
The first year we used it, we had a 10% gain

374
00:19:53,101 --> 00:19:57,800
in our planted acreage, simply because we were able to align

375
00:19:57,801 --> 00:20:03,500
our rows without any wasted ground in between the passes

376
00:20:03,501 --> 00:20:06,140
of our planter back and forth.

377
00:20:06,240 --> 00:20:08,640
That's significant.

378
00:20:08,740 --> 00:20:10,800
The GPS system also prevents farmers

379
00:20:10,900 --> 00:20:14,940
from accidentally steering their tractors over their crop,

380
00:20:15,040 --> 00:20:17,240
what farmers call greening.

381
00:20:17,340 --> 00:20:20,260
I would dare to say we've probably kicked 75, maybe even

382
00:20:20,360 --> 00:20:24,360
80% of our greening out of this crop.

383
00:20:24,460 --> 00:20:30,760
That's all product that now is usable as opposed to unusable.

384
00:20:30,860 --> 00:20:33,160
But technology can only solve part of the equation

385
00:20:33,161 --> 00:20:36,700
to running a family farm, meaning innovation doesn't

386
00:20:36,800 --> 00:20:38,500
stop at the potato field's edge.

387
00:20:41,800 --> 00:20:45,100
Our family has been in the potato industry in some way

388
00:20:45,200 --> 00:20:47,780
or another for five or six generations.

389
00:20:47,880 --> 00:20:50,680
So we always heard stories about turning potatoes

390
00:20:50,780 --> 00:20:51,840
into vodka.

391
00:20:51,940 --> 00:20:54,820
Fast track that to about five years ago,

392
00:20:54,920 --> 00:20:57,220
potato sales were down, and my brother

393
00:20:57,320 --> 00:21:00,680
was saying it's hard to make a living growing potatoes.

394
00:21:00,681 --> 00:21:03,820
potatoes, what about doing potato vodka?

395
00:21:03,920 --> 00:21:05,820
So we did.

396
00:21:05,920 --> 00:21:08,980
Fresh potatoes don't always look the way you'd like.

397
00:21:09,080 --> 00:21:13,620
Known as culls, these misfits are often destroyed.

398
00:21:13,720 --> 00:21:16,020
But at Cold River Vodka, they're turned

399
00:21:16,120 --> 00:21:18,340
into award-winning spirits.

400
00:21:18,440 --> 00:21:20,740
We've had potatoes that are 14 inches long

401
00:21:20,840 --> 00:21:23,040
and that are almost 5 inches thick.

402
00:21:23,140 --> 00:21:24,680
And they can't sell those for table stocking.

403
00:21:24,780 --> 00:21:26,580
Nobody wants them in a restaurant.

404
00:21:26,680 --> 00:21:30,680
The first year we went through about 800,000 pounds of potatoes.

405
00:21:30,681 --> 00:21:32,680
And all of those were culls.

406
00:21:32,780 --> 00:21:34,380
This year, we're probably going to be a little bit

407
00:21:34,480 --> 00:21:35,920
over a million pounds.

408
00:21:36,020 --> 00:21:40,320
We do anywhere from 25,000 to 30,000 pounds a week.

409
00:21:40,420 --> 00:21:41,820
The world's most common vodkas

410
00:21:41,920 --> 00:21:45,320
are made with wheat and rye.

411
00:21:45,420 --> 00:21:48,660
At Cold River, it takes 15 pounds of potatoes

412
00:21:48,760 --> 00:21:51,360
to make one bottle of vodka.

413
00:21:51,460 --> 00:21:55,680
The first step, mash more than 6,500 pounds of potatoes

414
00:21:55,780 --> 00:21:59,580
into a 1,500-gallon soup.

415
00:21:59,581 --> 00:22:03,780
Potatoes are 81% to 85% water, and the rest of it's starch

416
00:22:03,880 --> 00:22:05,080
and a few other things.

417
00:22:05,180 --> 00:22:08,420
We want to take that starch and convert that starch to sugar.

418
00:22:08,520 --> 00:22:11,120
And we do that by mashing them up and boiling.

419
00:22:11,220 --> 00:22:15,220
We have to take those starch molecules and break them down.

420
00:22:15,320 --> 00:22:18,940
Yeast is added, and the fermentation begins.

421
00:22:19,040 --> 00:22:22,300
Enzymes within the yeast quickly consume the potato sugar,

422
00:22:22,400 --> 00:22:25,640
generating alcohol, CO2, and heat.

423
00:22:25,740 --> 00:22:28,380
The potato soup is going to be in here for anywhere from 24

424
00:22:28,381 --> 00:22:29,540
to 36 hours.

425
00:22:29,541 --> 00:22:31,320
Then we'll have what we call a potato wine.

426
00:22:31,321 --> 00:22:33,020
And we just call it a potato wine

427
00:22:33,021 --> 00:22:36,320
because it has 8% to 9.5% alcohol.

428
00:22:36,321 --> 00:22:41,020
It's still the ugly old potato milkshake that we start with,

429
00:22:41,021 --> 00:22:42,820
but now it has alcohol, so it's what we're

430
00:22:42,821 --> 00:22:45,240
going to make our vodka.

431
00:22:45,241 --> 00:22:47,640
To separate the alcohol from the solids,

432
00:22:47,641 --> 00:22:51,640
the wine now flows into the copper distillation unit.

433
00:22:51,641 --> 00:22:54,740
When brought to a boil, distinct elements within the wine

434
00:22:54,741 --> 00:22:58,380
turn to gas, rise within the cooling tower,

435
00:22:58,381 --> 00:23:01,520
then condense into separate liquids.

436
00:23:01,620 --> 00:23:03,980
Most prominent among them is ethanol,

437
00:23:04,080 --> 00:23:06,020
vodka's primary ingredient.

438
00:23:06,120 --> 00:23:07,220
This is the soup.

439
00:23:07,320 --> 00:23:11,320
This is the first bit that we put in the pot still.

440
00:23:11,420 --> 00:23:15,400
And this is after the second distillation.

441
00:23:15,500 --> 00:23:17,140
This is our second distillate.

442
00:23:17,240 --> 00:23:22,740
This is about 9% alcohol, and this is roughly 95% alcohol.

443
00:23:22,840 --> 00:23:24,940
Cold River repeats the distillation process

444
00:23:25,040 --> 00:23:28,380
three times to create a more refined and pure alcohol.

445
00:23:28,381 --> 00:23:31,980
Then they blend it with filtered water from the nearby Cold River

446
00:23:31,981 --> 00:23:34,080
aquifer before bottling.

447
00:23:34,081 --> 00:23:35,580
It's not overly sweet.

448
00:23:35,581 --> 00:23:37,180
It's very, very subtle.

449
00:23:37,181 --> 00:23:40,520
There are some sugars in the potato that remain

450
00:23:40,521 --> 00:23:43,220
through fermentation and distillation

451
00:23:43,221 --> 00:23:45,440
that give it that subtle sweetness.

452
00:23:45,441 --> 00:23:47,340
It's got a very distinctive nose.

453
00:23:47,341 --> 00:23:49,340
It's got a lot of character.

454
00:23:49,341 --> 00:23:52,440
And for that, as well as the opportunities it creates,

455
00:23:52,441 --> 00:23:55,340
there's reason to celebrate.

456
00:23:55,341 --> 00:23:56,340
Good going, you guys.

457
00:23:56,341 --> 00:23:57,440
Cheers.

458
00:23:57,441 --> 00:23:58,340
Cheers.

459
00:23:58,440 --> 00:23:59,140
Thank you.

460
00:23:59,141 --> 00:24:00,040
Cheers.

461
00:24:00,140 --> 00:24:02,240
Of course, more potatoes wind up in this form

462
00:24:02,340 --> 00:24:03,880
than in vodka.

463
00:24:03,980 --> 00:24:08,280
But you have a challenger, French fry, the mighty tot.

464
00:24:12,380 --> 00:24:15,700
In 1872, plant wizard Luther Burbank

465
00:24:15,800 --> 00:24:19,000
found a single mutation in his potato garden.

466
00:24:19,100 --> 00:24:21,100
He planted it the following season

467
00:24:21,200 --> 00:24:24,340
and grew 23 new potato plants.

468
00:24:24,440 --> 00:24:27,440
They became the foundation for the russet burbank.

469
00:24:27,441 --> 00:24:29,440
America's most planted potato.

470
00:24:38,180 --> 00:24:40,980
Temptation comes in many forms, but rarely

471
00:24:41,080 --> 00:24:43,480
is it as seductive as the French fry.

472
00:24:43,580 --> 00:24:45,000
I think even if you're on a diet,

473
00:24:45,100 --> 00:24:48,000
you can't turn down French fries.

474
00:24:48,100 --> 00:24:50,100
Americans eat an average of four servings

475
00:24:50,200 --> 00:24:52,600
of French fries a week.

476
00:24:52,700 --> 00:24:54,600
So if you plan to be in this business,

477
00:24:54,601 --> 00:24:57,600
you'd better have a lot of spuds on hand.

478
00:24:57,601 --> 00:25:00,600
And no one stores more than Orida potatoes

479
00:25:00,601 --> 00:25:02,600
in Ontario, Oregon.

480
00:25:02,601 --> 00:25:04,600
The place is gigantic.

481
00:25:04,601 --> 00:25:06,600
We're standing here in one of our potato storage cellars,

482
00:25:06,601 --> 00:25:09,600
and this cellar covers about one acre of ground

483
00:25:09,601 --> 00:25:13,600
and is about 20 feet deep, stacked with potatoes.

484
00:25:13,601 --> 00:25:16,600
That's about 33 million pounds of potatoes,

485
00:25:16,601 --> 00:25:18,600
which equates to about 10 days' worth of usage

486
00:25:18,601 --> 00:25:20,600
for our factory.

487
00:25:20,601 --> 00:25:23,600
That's right, 33 million pounds in 10 days.

488
00:25:23,601 --> 00:25:25,600
which means in a year...

489
00:25:25,601 --> 00:25:28,600
We use over 1 billion pounds of raw potatoes

490
00:25:28,601 --> 00:25:29,600
going through our factory.

491
00:25:29,601 --> 00:25:31,600
The 1 billion pounds of potatoes that we have

492
00:25:31,601 --> 00:25:34,600
turns into about 640 to 700 million pounds

493
00:25:34,601 --> 00:25:37,600
of finished product each year, which is spread across a variety

494
00:25:37,601 --> 00:25:40,600
of different types of cuts that we make here in the plant.

495
00:25:40,601 --> 00:25:44,600
Stroll any frozen food aisle and Oreita products are there,

496
00:25:44,601 --> 00:25:46,600
from hash browns to mashed potatoes

497
00:25:46,601 --> 00:25:50,600
to their number one seller, french fries.

498
00:25:50,601 --> 00:25:55,000
Arita sells about 500 million pounds of French fries a year.

499
00:25:55,100 --> 00:25:56,340
The trick?

500
00:25:56,440 --> 00:25:58,540
The look and flavor can't waver.

501
00:25:58,640 --> 00:26:01,240
After all, we take our French fries seriously.

502
00:26:01,340 --> 00:26:04,240
To make a consistent fry, any manufacturer

503
00:26:04,340 --> 00:26:07,960
begins with uniform-sized and shaped potatoes that

504
00:26:08,060 --> 00:26:11,460
are sliced into standard lengths and forms.

505
00:26:11,560 --> 00:26:15,000
Each day, tens of thousands of potatoes enter a water line

506
00:26:15,100 --> 00:26:18,500
that flows rapidly through a long tube,

507
00:26:18,501 --> 00:26:21,140
Traveling now at 30 miles per hour,

508
00:26:21,240 --> 00:26:26,100
the potato surges through an iron mesh as sharp as ninja steel.

509
00:26:26,200 --> 00:26:28,780
What was once whole is now sliced

510
00:26:28,880 --> 00:26:31,980
into more than 20 perfect fries.

511
00:26:32,080 --> 00:26:34,420
But hidden within this Cadillac process

512
00:26:34,520 --> 00:26:39,620
is the genesis of another Cinderella story, the tater tot,

513
00:26:39,720 --> 00:26:42,820
born from the scraps and orphans that the French fry process

514
00:26:42,920 --> 00:26:43,920
leaves behind.

515
00:26:44,020 --> 00:26:46,020
When we make French fries, we're

516
00:26:46,021 --> 00:26:51,020
we're left with a product we call shorts and slivers,

517
00:26:51,120 --> 00:26:55,020
which are not suitable for grade in regular french fries.

518
00:26:55,120 --> 00:26:57,160
This, in the early days, was being thrown away,

519
00:26:57,260 --> 00:26:58,460
and it was quite a waste.

520
00:26:58,560 --> 00:27:00,360
So people put their heads together

521
00:27:00,460 --> 00:27:02,540
and came up with this idea of cutting this up

522
00:27:02,640 --> 00:27:06,180
in smaller pieces and forming it into a cylindrical shape

523
00:27:06,280 --> 00:27:07,180
and frying it.

524
00:27:07,280 --> 00:27:10,180
Thus, tater tots were born.

525
00:27:10,280 --> 00:27:11,980
Transforming that potato material

526
00:27:12,080 --> 00:27:16,020
into everyone's favorite tot is fairly spud-tastic.

527
00:27:16,021 --> 00:27:18,520
spectacular.

528
00:27:18,620 --> 00:27:21,560
This production line runs at 15,000 to 20,000 pounds

529
00:27:21,660 --> 00:27:24,860
of tots per hour.

530
00:27:24,960 --> 00:27:27,460
So after a quick blanch, these slivers and misfits

531
00:27:27,560 --> 00:27:30,360
are ready to be formed.

532
00:27:30,460 --> 00:27:32,500
Behind me, the tater tot material

533
00:27:32,600 --> 00:27:36,680
is combined with various dry ingredients mixed up

534
00:27:36,780 --> 00:27:41,480
and then inserted into a rotating former drum.

535
00:27:41,580 --> 00:27:45,080
The potato mixture presses against the rotating drum

536
00:27:45,081 --> 00:27:49,580
into small barrel-shaped openings as the drum spins.

537
00:27:49,680 --> 00:27:52,920
With the mixture now in the classic tater tot shape,

538
00:27:53,020 --> 00:27:57,200
pistons inside the drum force the tots outward and onto the next

539
00:27:57,300 --> 00:28:00,260
step in the process.

540
00:28:00,360 --> 00:28:04,200
Those tater tots are then put through the fryer behind me

541
00:28:04,300 --> 00:28:06,500
and then on into the freezer.

542
00:28:06,600 --> 00:28:10,200
The process moves like clockwork, fast and efficient.

543
00:28:10,300 --> 00:28:12,640
Now freshly fried, the tots leap from the oil

544
00:28:12,641 --> 00:28:15,980
into a freezer set at minus 20 degrees Fahrenheit.

545
00:28:16,080 --> 00:28:18,280
Then they're packaged and shipped.

546
00:28:18,380 --> 00:28:20,980
One of our leading sales products is tater tots,

547
00:28:21,080 --> 00:28:24,780
and we sell about 3.6 billion tater tots per year.

548
00:28:24,880 --> 00:28:26,720
If you put all those tater tots end to end,

549
00:28:26,820 --> 00:28:30,400
they would actually circle the globe roughly three times.

550
00:28:30,500 --> 00:28:32,500
But not all potato products are formed

551
00:28:32,600 --> 00:28:34,500
on such a grand scale.

552
00:28:34,600 --> 00:28:38,900
And sometimes variety truly is the spice of life.

553
00:28:39,000 --> 00:28:41,140
This is the variety All Blue.

554
00:28:41,141 --> 00:28:46,440
It's also known as purple marker, and it's a beautiful plant.

555
00:28:48,220 --> 00:28:49,560
There's a good-sized one.

556
00:28:50,800 --> 00:28:52,780
This is one of our latest varieties.

557
00:28:53,940 --> 00:28:55,120
Very dark blue skin.

558
00:28:56,240 --> 00:28:58,640
It's got a beautiful purple flesh.

559
00:29:00,040 --> 00:29:05,980
Clean them off, rub some olive oil on the skin, put them on a cookie sheet.

560
00:29:06,140 --> 00:29:09,560
They'd cook in 10 or 15 minutes, and then have some sour cream or butter,

561
00:29:09,561 --> 00:29:11,240
and you've got a meal in itself right there.

562
00:29:12,120 --> 00:29:15,240
Here at Wood Prairie Farms in Aroostook County, Maine,

563
00:29:16,120 --> 00:29:18,580
Jim and Megan Gerritsen run one of the leading specialty

564
00:29:18,581 --> 00:29:19,880
potato farms in the country.

565
00:29:20,920 --> 00:29:24,200
They produce 25 varieties of organically grown potatoes,

566
00:29:24,860 --> 00:29:28,080
and they come in many colors, textures, and flavors.

567
00:29:29,020 --> 00:29:30,660
This is cranberry red.

568
00:29:30,820 --> 00:29:32,860
It's a low-density potato.

569
00:29:34,940 --> 00:29:37,980
It's pink inside, and it's got a real following

570
00:29:37,981 --> 00:29:40,780
among some chefs that really like this one

571
00:29:40,781 --> 00:29:43,320
for texture and flavor.

572
00:29:43,321 --> 00:29:45,780
But it's not just a matter of taste.

573
00:29:45,781 --> 00:29:49,320
New research suggests real health benefits as well.

574
00:29:49,321 --> 00:29:53,720
There's a likely correlation between the brighter the color,

575
00:29:53,721 --> 00:29:56,140
you know, the more intense the color, like these purples,

576
00:29:56,141 --> 00:29:58,140
and the higher amount of antioxidants

577
00:29:58,141 --> 00:30:01,640
and some of the other health-giving compounds in potatoes.

578
00:30:01,641 --> 00:30:04,280
As a rule of thumb, the brighter-colored potatoes

579
00:30:04,281 --> 00:30:07,980
seem to have more nutrient density.

580
00:30:07,981 --> 00:30:10,520
Somewhere in the genetic codes of the world's many potato

581
00:30:10,620 --> 00:30:14,060
varieties is the key to stopping a centuries-old killer,

582
00:30:14,160 --> 00:30:15,160
late blight.

583
00:30:15,260 --> 00:30:19,460
I have seen personally fields go down in three or four days.

584
00:30:19,560 --> 00:30:21,520
All it takes is the presence of the disease

585
00:30:21,620 --> 00:30:25,760
and the right climate and susceptibility of the potato.

586
00:30:25,860 --> 00:30:27,460
Bam.

587
00:30:27,560 --> 00:30:31,240
Late blight is a merciless airborne fungus.

588
00:30:31,340 --> 00:30:34,600
In the 1840s, it ignited the Irish potato famine

589
00:30:34,700 --> 00:30:37,980
that left more than a million dead.

590
00:30:37,981 --> 00:30:41,580
It continues to destroy nearly 20% of the world's potato crop

591
00:30:41,680 --> 00:30:44,020
every year.

592
00:30:44,120 --> 00:30:46,620
This year, we happen to be dealing with it.

593
00:30:46,720 --> 00:30:47,720
It's everywhere.

594
00:30:47,820 --> 00:30:50,220
And what happens is it'll infect the potato leaves,

595
00:30:50,320 --> 00:30:52,720
then it'll infect the stalks, then it'll move down into the

596
00:30:52,820 --> 00:30:53,820
tubers.

597
00:30:53,920 --> 00:30:56,340
Once it infects the tubers, they can rot in storage,

598
00:30:56,440 --> 00:30:58,540
and they can rot right in the ground.

599
00:30:58,640 --> 00:31:01,640
Basically, it can annihilate your entire crop.

600
00:31:01,740 --> 00:31:04,340
For now, only diligence, chemical fungicides,

601
00:31:04,341 --> 00:31:08,140
fungicides and a little luck help farmers to battle late

602
00:31:08,141 --> 00:31:11,680
blight's potentially devastating consequences.

603
00:31:11,681 --> 00:31:14,480
But there is reason for hope.

604
00:31:14,481 --> 00:31:16,880
Here at the United States Department of Agriculture's

605
00:31:16,881 --> 00:31:19,420
Potato Research Center in Prosser, Washington,

606
00:31:19,421 --> 00:31:22,460
scientists like Chuck Brown cross and grow different potato

607
00:31:22,461 --> 00:31:25,460
varieties in search of disease resistance.

608
00:31:25,461 --> 00:31:28,100
Pollen from potentially blight-free potato plants

609
00:31:28,101 --> 00:31:31,640
collected around the world is wed to the genetic codes of more

610
00:31:31,641 --> 00:31:35,140
commercial, yet vulnerable varieties.

611
00:31:35,141 --> 00:31:39,140
It's a genetic labyrinth, and the project will take years.

612
00:31:39,141 --> 00:31:41,140
But progress has been made.

613
00:31:41,141 --> 00:31:45,240
We had a collaborative research project with a breeding program

614
00:31:45,241 --> 00:31:46,640
in Poland.

615
00:31:46,641 --> 00:31:50,540
And after a number of years, the work that they did resulted

616
00:31:50,541 --> 00:31:54,680
in a set of 10 clones that were highly late blight resistant.

617
00:31:54,681 --> 00:31:58,700
And they sent them to us, and we're maintaining these in vitro,

618
00:31:58,701 --> 00:32:01,340
and we use them in crossing.

619
00:32:01,341 --> 00:32:03,980
Had this rare seedling been the primary variety planted

620
00:32:03,981 --> 00:32:09,440
throughout Ireland in 1845, there would have been no famine.

621
00:32:09,441 --> 00:32:11,960
And if it can be crossed with more commercial varieties

622
00:32:11,961 --> 00:32:14,960
in the future, then the threat of late blight

623
00:32:14,961 --> 00:32:18,660
may finally be over.

624
00:32:18,661 --> 00:32:22,860
Got a guess on how to hide 20 million pounds of potatoes?

625
00:32:22,861 --> 00:32:24,360
Start by checking your freezer.

626
00:32:27,600 --> 00:32:29,960
In the 19th century, baked potatoes

627
00:32:29,961 --> 00:32:32,700
to factory workers on the late shift.

628
00:32:32,800 --> 00:32:35,360
Not only were these baked spuds nutritious,

629
00:32:35,460 --> 00:32:37,500
workers put them in their pockets

630
00:32:37,600 --> 00:32:39,000
to keep their hands warm.

631
00:32:48,220 --> 00:32:49,420
Here's a riddle.

632
00:32:49,520 --> 00:32:53,920
How do you get 300 pounds of potatoes into a 50-pound bag?

633
00:32:54,020 --> 00:32:56,420
The answer?

634
00:32:56,520 --> 00:32:59,020
Dehydration.

635
00:32:59,021 --> 00:33:02,720
The process that reduces the water content in fresh potatoes

636
00:33:02,820 --> 00:33:06,720
and allows you to turn them into potato flakes and flour.

637
00:33:06,820 --> 00:33:10,060
The industry calls it de-high, and you may recognize it

638
00:33:10,160 --> 00:33:11,960
as instant mashed potatoes.

639
00:33:12,060 --> 00:33:15,780
But did you know it's also in your ice cream, your soup,

640
00:33:15,880 --> 00:33:18,280
even your jelly beans?

641
00:33:18,380 --> 00:33:21,180
And those perfectly formed chips that come in a can,

642
00:33:21,280 --> 00:33:23,120
they're de-high too.

643
00:33:23,220 --> 00:33:25,520
Because when you suck the water out of potatoes,

644
00:33:25,620 --> 00:33:29,020
what's left can be made into any shape you want.

645
00:33:29,021 --> 00:33:34,020
including a world record center.

646
00:33:34,120 --> 00:33:36,860
No one does this better than the folks at Keystone Potato

647
00:33:36,960 --> 00:33:40,160
Products in Higgins, Pennsylvania.

648
00:33:40,260 --> 00:33:42,860
They operate one of the most advanced potato dehydration

649
00:33:42,960 --> 00:33:47,780
systems in the world, capable of producing more than 20 million

650
00:33:47,880 --> 00:33:49,880
pounds of dehydrated potatoes a year.

651
00:33:53,880 --> 00:33:55,280
And here's the thing.

652
00:33:55,380 --> 00:33:59,020
The entire production line is completely automated.

653
00:33:59,021 --> 00:34:02,320
This is one of two control panels in the processing room.

654
00:34:02,420 --> 00:34:05,820
This entire line is automated from touch screen panels.

655
00:34:05,920 --> 00:34:09,160
In this room, we only have two people running the line.

656
00:34:09,260 --> 00:34:12,360
So from this control panel, this one of two control panels,

657
00:34:12,460 --> 00:34:16,780
we can control everything that happens in this room.

658
00:34:16,880 --> 00:34:19,700
Almost like a giant robot, the Keystone system

659
00:34:19,800 --> 00:34:24,720
processes more than 18,000 pounds of potatoes an hour.

660
00:34:24,820 --> 00:34:27,420
It begins with the peel.

661
00:34:27,421 --> 00:34:30,260
The first step in the dehydration process

662
00:34:30,360 --> 00:34:33,260
is to actually remove the peel from the potato.

663
00:34:33,360 --> 00:34:35,660
And we do that with steam.

664
00:34:35,760 --> 00:34:38,260
A steam peeler subjects each batch of potatoes

665
00:34:38,360 --> 00:34:42,060
to high pressure steam for 10 to 20 seconds,

666
00:34:42,160 --> 00:34:44,480
loosening the peel.

667
00:34:44,580 --> 00:34:49,080
Brushes then scrub the peel off before a powerful slicer

668
00:34:49,180 --> 00:34:51,280
cuts the potato into half-inch slices.

669
00:34:53,480 --> 00:34:56,520
These slices are briefly heated, then rapidly

670
00:34:56,521 --> 00:35:00,520
then rapidly cooled in a cold water immersion.

671
00:35:00,620 --> 00:35:02,620
Our objective here is to get the potatoes

672
00:35:02,720 --> 00:35:07,560
from 165 degrees down to 65 degrees as quickly as possible.

673
00:35:07,660 --> 00:35:10,660
And it's almost like a heat treatment process for metal,

674
00:35:10,760 --> 00:35:14,600
where we are affecting the potato cells and strengthening

675
00:35:14,700 --> 00:35:17,500
them and preventing them from bursting when they proceed

676
00:35:17,600 --> 00:35:21,720
to the next step, which is the cooking process.

677
00:35:21,820 --> 00:35:25,540
The potatoes cook for less than half an hour.

678
00:35:25,541 --> 00:35:28,280
And that's when things get sticky.

679
00:35:28,380 --> 00:35:30,380
After the cooker, they make their way

680
00:35:30,480 --> 00:35:34,280
to one of two drying drums, which takes that mashed product

681
00:35:34,380 --> 00:35:37,580
now that you see, and that product that adheres to that

682
00:35:37,680 --> 00:35:41,480
drum, and on the other side, the dry product then comes off

683
00:35:41,580 --> 00:35:45,400
in the form of potato flake or sheets of, almost looks like

684
00:35:45,500 --> 00:35:47,900
sheets of paper.

685
00:35:48,000 --> 00:35:51,500
Pressure combined with heat from within the large drums

686
00:35:51,600 --> 00:35:55,540
forces the potatoes to lose nearly all their moisture.

687
00:35:55,541 --> 00:35:58,780
The mash product comes onto the drum at anywhere between 80%

688
00:35:58,880 --> 00:36:00,840
and 90% moisture.

689
00:36:00,940 --> 00:36:02,580
Our objective when it leaves the drum

690
00:36:02,680 --> 00:36:08,480
is to have the moisture level anywhere between 6% and 8%.

691
00:36:08,580 --> 00:36:12,660
Those paper-thin sheets are run over a shaker table

692
00:36:12,760 --> 00:36:14,300
and then through an optical sorter

693
00:36:14,400 --> 00:36:17,800
to remove even the smallest defects.

694
00:36:17,900 --> 00:36:19,500
After the optical sorter, the product

695
00:36:19,600 --> 00:36:23,460
goes into a grinder, a hammer mill, where we size the product,

696
00:36:23,461 --> 00:36:27,300
convey it pneumatically into the silo.

697
00:36:27,400 --> 00:36:28,860
That feeds the bagger.

698
00:36:28,960 --> 00:36:32,400
Bags are filled to roughly 40 pounds each.

699
00:36:32,500 --> 00:36:35,340
We take those bags, we seal them with a heat sealer,

700
00:36:35,440 --> 00:36:36,640
put them through a metal detector,

701
00:36:36,740 --> 00:36:38,040
and then stack them on the bags, and they're

702
00:36:38,140 --> 00:36:41,140
ready to go out to the customer.

703
00:36:41,240 --> 00:36:43,480
Only at the very end do human hands

704
00:36:43,580 --> 00:36:46,520
play a part in this highly automated process,

705
00:36:46,620 --> 00:36:48,420
which can customize the flake or flower

706
00:36:48,520 --> 00:36:52,360
to meet any grade required by the customer.

707
00:36:52,361 --> 00:36:55,020
Remarkably, little of the fresh potato's nutritional value

708
00:36:55,120 --> 00:36:57,600
is lost along the way.

709
00:36:57,700 --> 00:37:00,760
But if you think that's news, consider

710
00:37:00,860 --> 00:37:04,900
that dehydrating potatoes dates back thousands of years.

711
00:37:05,000 --> 00:37:08,080
Ancient South Americans stepped on potatoes, crushing the water

712
00:37:08,180 --> 00:37:09,580
from them.

713
00:37:09,680 --> 00:37:13,020
The remains were called chunyo and were stored for years

714
00:37:13,120 --> 00:37:16,680
as a hedge against famine.

715
00:37:16,780 --> 00:37:19,020
Sadly, famine, hunger, and malnutrition

716
00:37:19,120 --> 00:37:22,360
remain a challenge even today.

717
00:37:22,361 --> 00:37:25,760
Fortunately, the very characteristics that make DEHI so

718
00:37:25,860 --> 00:37:28,760
valuable in the commercial marketplace also make it

719
00:37:28,860 --> 00:37:32,400
extremely desirable for relief organizations, like the San

720
00:37:32,500 --> 00:37:36,400
Diego-based charity, Project Concerned International.

721
00:37:36,500 --> 00:37:39,720
They provide a high caloric intake, small amount of food

722
00:37:39,820 --> 00:37:43,420
that really provide high nutritional value to children.

723
00:37:43,520 --> 00:37:47,060
In this particular program, the US Potato Board provided

724
00:37:47,160 --> 00:37:51,420
dehydrated potatoes, and one of the benefits to that particular

725
00:37:51,421 --> 00:37:54,720
that particular commodity or source of food over others

726
00:37:54,721 --> 00:37:57,180
is it's lightweight because there's transportation costs

727
00:37:57,181 --> 00:37:58,900
that are related to sending the food

728
00:37:58,901 --> 00:38:01,060
from the United States overseas.

729
00:38:03,120 --> 00:38:04,980
Each year, Project Concern

730
00:38:04,981 --> 00:38:06,540
and other international aid efforts

731
00:38:06,541 --> 00:38:08,900
distribute more than 10 million pounds

732
00:38:08,901 --> 00:38:11,080
of dehydrated potatoes to those in need,

733
00:38:11,700 --> 00:38:14,200
a vital role for this humble tuber.

734
00:38:16,920 --> 00:38:18,900
Meanwhile, be it flake or flour,

735
00:38:19,280 --> 00:38:20,840
the potato can hold its own.

736
00:38:21,420 --> 00:38:23,420
But what about its firepower?

737
00:38:23,520 --> 00:38:26,420
Here's a spud that can really pack a punch.

738
00:38:26,520 --> 00:38:29,920
Oh, nailed it.

739
00:38:30,020 --> 00:38:31,960
No one eats more potatoes than folks

740
00:38:32,060 --> 00:38:34,960
in the Republic of Belarus, formerly part of the Soviet

741
00:38:35,060 --> 00:38:35,860
Union.

742
00:38:35,960 --> 00:38:40,480
The country consumes 397 pounds of potatoes per capita,

743
00:38:40,580 --> 00:38:44,780
over a pound of potatoes a day for every man, woman, and child.

744
00:38:51,420 --> 00:38:56,420
Before the Slinky or Legos or even Barbie was on the air,

745
00:38:56,421 --> 00:38:58,420
there was Mr. Potato Head,

746
00:38:58,421 --> 00:39:03,420
the first child's toy ever advertised on TV.

747
